## Summary

Security audit of main at 46e759d across the whole codebase: sign-in, sessions, bearer tokens, Spoof Mode, every public path (hooks, `/open/`, `/ext/`, MCP OAuth), every `/api/do/` handler, uploads, blob serving, outbound fetches, mail in and out, the full policy source, Ask and MCP tools, and the browser's HTML and link sinks under the CSP.

One real issue fixed: two Birthdays policy clauses let a Birthdays admin add or remove members of any group the Birthdays Viewers rules name, with no limit on the target's kind, and in production those rules name the `birthday-admins` admins group itself, so non-super-admin Birthdays admins could appoint or remove Birthdays admins. The app's docs described that reach while the data model's rule says only an admins group's managers (the super admins) change it; ian chose the model's rule. The clauses now exclude admins groups and role groups, `TestOnlySuperAdminsKeepTheBirthdaysAdmins` holds it (checked to fail against main's policy, after a review finding that its first version failed for the wrong reason), and the Birthdays sentences in `docs/datamodel.md` and `docs/birthday/birthday.md` match. Cost: Birthdays admins who are not super admins ask one to change the admin list; they keep the volunteers and the Comms Team as before.

Deliberately left alone: Team admins' whole-address redirects (documented, admins doing their job); unsubscribe tokens signed with the raw session key (distinct payload shapes keep them apart, and re-keying breaks every link in sent mail); unbounded invitations by a host (the product's hosting model); Loop keeping unauthenticated posts before dropping them (documented); `GET /open/ext/{token}` recording "opened" (token holder only); dev tooling exposures (documented in the audit doc). An app admin re-pointing an event's managers at an unrelated group is already refused by `authorizeServingGroups` (issue #200).

